    "key_arguments_for_B": [
      "Fluidic shear vs. conjugate stability: The BD FACSDiscover S8 images upstream in the cuvette flow cell prior to nozzle passage and droplet formation. Weak, random doublets readily dissociate under high shear stress (~70 psi) between interrogation and droplet deflection, so only the tumor cell (or no cell) lands in the well, yielding no B-cell template in 91 wells.",
      "Precursor frequency: True antigen-specific B cells against tumor cells are rare (<0.1%), so physically imaged doublets without selection are overwhelmingly random, mechanically fragile associations.",
      "Stem-sentence relevance: The prompt's final sentence noting 'cultured 40 min prior to staining on ice for 30 min' highlights conditions that fail to stabilize low-affinity/random doublets (cold disrupts active conjugate stabilization), further explaining why only true high-affinity conjugates stayed intact."
    ],
    "rebuttals_against_E": [
      "Quantitative biochemistry: A single tumor cell contains 10-30 pg of total RNA. SuperScript III in standard single-cell RT (even in 5 µL) is formulated to reverse-transcribe up to microgram amounts with millimolar dNTPs and picomoles of primers. Picograms of competitor RNA cannot biochemically exhaust reagents or shut down RT-PCR to a 4/95 failure rate.",
      "Stem irrelevance: If E were correct, the specific incubation and staining parameters stated at the end would be completely irrelevant."
    ],
    "rebuttals_against_C_and_D": [
      "C incorrectly claims conditions led to 'few doublets'—contradicting the fact that 95 doublet wells were successfully sorted.",
      "D incorrectly specifies 'alpha and beta chains' (TCR, not BCR)."
    ]
  },
  "next_step": "Maintain support for B, highlighting both the fluidic shear dissociation mechanism (explaining the missing gel bands) and the stoichiometric implausibility of E."
}

